Anthropic: New safety framework "Enterprise Frontier Safeguards" combines zero data retention with abuse detection, keeping monitoring data in the customer's cloud rather than with Anthropic — supports Claude Code, Enterprise, Bedrock, Google Agent Platform, and Microsoft Foundry .

52
00:08:11,761 --> 00:08:32,136
Media law: Seattle Times and Newsday are suing OpenAI and Microsoft for copyright infringement, despite prior journalism funding from Microsoft/OpenAI — a quote from the lawsuit: "AI products like ChatGPT and CoPilot are touted as producers of content, but in fact they are rapacious consumers." .

53
00:08:32,136 --> 00:08:45,636
Funding: Accel is reportedly negotiating a $1B round in Mira Murati's Thinking Machines, at a $40B valuation — another example of the extreme funding pace in the AI sector .

54
00:08:45,636 --> 00:08:46,761
Tool of the Week.

55
00:08:46,761 --> 00:08:51,349
Google Gemini Spark — now manages your own Google Photos library.

56
00:08:51,458 --> 00:09:03,495
Gemini Spark, Google's personal agent, can now edit photos, curate albums, automatically create shared albums with favorites, and even turn concert posters directly into calendar entries .

57
00:09:03,495 --> 00:09:08,933
Access is simple: connect Google Photos, enable Spark, type an instruction.

58
00:09:08,933 --> 00:09:18,145
Rollout is initially US-only (English) for Gemini AI Pro/Ultra subscribers, with no international timeline yet announced .

59
00:09:18,145 --> 00:09:30,708
Google Photos lead Shimrit Ben-Yair once dreamed of "having a power agent to help me get the most out of my 143,206 photos and videos"  — this week that became reality for her.

60
00:09:30,708 --> 00:09:42,095
Especially useful for anyone with years of chaotically managed photo libraries: incremental rather than revolutionary, but a concrete, immediately usable everyday tool.

61
00:09:42,095 --> 00:09:43,283
Google Photos

62
00:09:43,283 --> 00:09:44,433
Fail of the Week.

63
00:09:44,433 --> 00:09:49,270
"Advised by Gemini to bring far less food and water than their group required"

64
00:09:49,270 --> 00:09:54,495
Three young men used Google Gemini to plan a hike on Mount Shasta in California.

65
00:09:54,495 --> 00:10:01,408
Start time 3:00 a.m., planned duration 8 hours, actual summit arrival not until 7:00 p.m. .

66
00:10:01,408 --> 00:10:14,708
The group significantly exceeded the recommended 12:00 p.m. turnaround time, kept hiking into darkness, and ultimately had to spend the night in Mud Creek Canyon before the Forest Service and volunteers rescued them the next morning .

67
00:10:14,708 --> 00:10:29,445
Root Cause: According to the Sheriff's Office, the group was "advised by Gemini to bring far less food and water than their group required"  — the model systematically underestimated the actual time and resource needs of a wilderness-capable hike.

68
00:10:29,445 --> 00:10:47,958
What we learn: For safety-critical everyday planning (wilderness navigation, gear, time management), never rely solely on an AI model — the official warning explicitly states "never rely solely on AI for your trip planning," and recommends contacting the local ranger station beforehand instead .

69
00:10:47,958 --> 00:10:49,095
Number of the Week.

70
